Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text into html formatted text:

```c
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;
    std::istringstream iss(markdown);
    std::string line;
    while (std::getline(iss, line)) {
        html += "<p>" + line + "</p>";
    }
    return html;
}
```

This function accepts a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing html formatted text. The markdown text is processed line by line, and each line is wrapped in a `<p>` tag to create an html paragraph. The resulting html string is then returned.

Here's an example usage:

```c
int main() {
    std::string markdown = "# Hello, World!\nThis is another paragraph.\nThis is another one!";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This will output:

```
<p>Hello, World!</p>
<p>This is another paragraph.</p>
<p>This is another one!</p>
```

